WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:05.700
Hello and welcome to this video on Blend Spaces.

2
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:12.199
A Blend Space is a tool that allows us to smoothly transition between different animations

3
00:00:12.199 --> 00:00:21.199
based on some kind of variable or input, like camera speed, direction, or a custom parameter if you need it.

4
00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:29.699
This is particularly useful for creating realistic character movements, like transitions from walking to running,

5
00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:33.700
which we will be doing in this session.

6
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:43.200
So, here we are back in the project, and I'm in the My Animation Blueprint directory that we will be working in.

7
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.700
We're going to create a new asset.

8
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:52.700
So if you right-click and you come up into the Animation section,

9
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.700
you'll see that we have an option for Blend Space.

10
00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:02.060
So if you select this, it's going to create a new asset.

11
00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:06.060
Again, we're going to be asked which skeleton that we want to use.

12
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.060
We are using the SK underscore mannequin.

13
00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:15.250
So I'm going to select the SK mannequin, and we have our Blend Space.

14
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:22.879
I'll name this My BS for Blend Space Walk Run.

15
00:01:22.879 --> 00:01:31.519
I'm going to double-click on this Blend Space.

16
00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:38.319
And this should bring up a window that looks a little bit like this.

17
00:01:38.319 --> 00:01:43.739
Now we have the asset details down the left-hand side.

18
00:01:43.739 --> 00:01:47.239
This will allow us to control how the Blend Space is set up.

19
00:01:47.239 --> 00:01:49.769
We have a viewport in the middle.

20
00:01:49.769 --> 00:01:55.909
This will allow us to preview the animation as it is being blended.

21
00:01:55.909 --> 00:01:58.909
Underneath the viewport is a grid.

22
00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:04.739
We can drag the animations onto this in order to blend them.

23
00:02:04.739 --> 00:02:08.740
And in the bottom right-hand corner, we have an asset browser.

24
00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:13.030
This will appear in numerous different places around the engine.

25
00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:21.849
The main thing to understand is this will only display animations for the selected skeleton.

26
00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:28.849
What we're going to do to start off with is we're going to be working only with the horizontal axis.

27
00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:33.849
So we're going to start off by renaming this horizontal axis.

28
00:02:33.849 --> 00:02:36.849
And we're going to change this to be Speed.

29
00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:42.750
And once we've got that, we're going to change the maximum axis value.

30
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:48.319
Because we're going to input the speed that the character is moving at any one time

31
00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:51.319
in order to determine the output animation.

32
00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:56.639
So the maximum speed we're going to go up to in this is going to be 500.

33
00:02:56.639 --> 00:03:05.150
So 0 is going to be our minimum axis value and 500 is going to be our maximum axis value.

34
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:12.800
Now, once we've done that, let's grab some animations.

35
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.800
So the first one that I want is going to be an idle.

36
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.800
So I'm going to search for idle.

37
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.319
And I've got a couple of options here.

38
00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:24.319
I'm going to go with the top one.

39
00:03:24.319 --> 00:03:27.379
So mm underscore idle.

40
00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:29.379
I'm going to drag that in.

41
00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:34.729
And I'm going to place that all the way over here to the left.

42
00:03:34.729 --> 00:03:38.120
That's going to be in the bottom left hand corner.

43
00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.120
And we're not going to be using the vertical axis.

44
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.120
So we don't need to worry about it in this instance.

45
00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.120
We could place this at any point and it wouldn't really matter.

46
00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.009
Now, next up, I'm going to select Run.

47
00:03:53.009 --> 00:04:00.750
And you can see that we have mm underscore run underscore forward in our Asset Browser.

48
00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:06.780
I will drag that in and I'm going to place that right down at 500.

49
00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:14.030
Now, we have two keys, as you can see, as the small little diamonds on our grid.

50
00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:18.029
But we also have this green cross.

51
00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:23.089
That green cross is going to determine what animation we are previewing.

52
00:04:23.089 --> 00:04:28.379
And to move that, we need to push the control key.

53
00:04:28.379 --> 00:04:32.379
And that will move the green cross to wherever your cursor is at that moment.

54
00:04:32.379 --> 00:04:37.379
So you can see if I hold this down, as I move it to the left,

55
00:04:37.379 --> 00:04:39.439
it slows down and goes back to the idle.

56
00:04:39.439 --> 00:04:43.439
And as I move it to the right, it speeds up to the run.

57
00:04:43.439 --> 00:04:47.829
As you can see in the middle here, this isn't a terribly natural movement.

58
00:04:47.829 --> 00:04:50.829
So let's add in one more animation.

59
00:04:50.829 --> 00:04:52.829
Let's find walk.

60
00:04:52.829 --> 00:04:59.120
You can see again, we have mm underscore walk underscore forward.

61
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.180
We'll drag that down and place that as close to the center as possible.

62
00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:07.720
You can see that gives us a good walk cycle.

63
00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:11.759
Now, I move the cursor around left and right.

64
00:05:11.759 --> 00:05:17.980
We go from no movement all the way up to running with a walk in the middle.

65
00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:32.529
So now I'm going to save this asset of my BS walk run.

66
00:05:32.529 --> 00:05:35.560
And that's everything we need to do there.

67
00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:39.560
We can drag this in and we can start using this at this point.

68
00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:43.550
So let's have a look at connecting this up.

69
00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:49.060
And to do that, I am going to jump back into the browser

70
00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:53.060
and I'm going to select my animation BP.

71
00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:59.490
Now, this is the first time that we've looked at the animation blueprint in detail.

72
00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.490
So let's go over the different elements.

73
00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:09.199
First of all, we have a viewport up in the top left hand corner here.

74
00:06:09.199 --> 00:06:16.980
And this again is going to preview the animations.

75
00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:21.980
In the center, we actually have the AmiGraph at this point.

76
00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:27.110
Now, you can navigate around this by holding down the right hand mouse button

77
00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:32.110
and you can drag and move the canvas around.

78
00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:35.110
And again, much like any of the other blueprints,

79
00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:39.110
if you right-hand click, you can search for nodes to add into it.

80
00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:45.899
So for example, if I was to add in a boolean,

81
00:06:45.899 --> 00:06:51.410
I would be able to drag in and connect up different elements like this.

82
00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.509
We'll be working on this in a while.

83
00:06:53.509 --> 00:06:57.600
The other tab that we have here is the event graph.

84
00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.639
This event graph, as we've talked about earlier,

85
00:06:59.639 --> 00:07:03.639
this is going to be where we will bring in different values

86
00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:07.639
and we will process them in order to figure out the animation.

87
00:07:07.639 --> 00:07:13.639
So the event graph is the logic that will process the variables.

88
00:07:13.639 --> 00:07:19.800
The animation is going to be where we will plug in different animations

89
00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.990
in order to create the output pose.

90
00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:26.990
So as you can see here, we start off with just a singular node

91
00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:28.990
and that is the output pose.

92
00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:32.990
And whatever is being put into this

93
00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:37.180
is going to be what is played on the animation.

94
00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:41.180
So let's have a look in the bottom right hand corner

95
00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:44.370
where we have the asset browser again.

96
00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:48.370
Any of these animations are going to work directly on this skeleton.

97
00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:51.370
So if I grab in the idle, drag it in,

98
00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.370
you can see that we now have a node for that idle.

99
00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:57.370
When I left-hand click on the little man,

100
00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.370
I could plug that directly into the output pose.

101
00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:03.660
And if I hit the compile button,

102
00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.660
you can see it's going to display that animation.

103
00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:09.660
So you can see it processing and running the animation here.

104
00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:14.139
And this would work. So if I was to save and compile,

105
00:08:14.139 --> 00:08:16.139
run into the level and run,

106
00:08:16.139 --> 00:08:19.139
you can see now I have my character idling.

107
00:08:19.139 --> 00:08:24.170
However, they're not playing any other animation when we move around.

108
00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.170
So that's what we want to fix here.

109
00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:32.799
Jump back in. I'm going to delete this idle.

110
00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:37.039
And to start with, I'm going to use a state machine.

111
00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.039
So let's create one and we'll discuss what it is.

112
00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:41.039
So if you right-hand click,

113
00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:44.039
and you search for state machine,

114
00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:50.220
you'll see there is an option that comes up here that says state machine.

115
00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:53.759
And I'm going to select this state machine.

116
00:08:53.759 --> 00:08:58.049
Now by default, it starts off being called the new state machine.

117
00:08:58.049 --> 00:09:03.919
But let's give it a name so we understand what animations this is outputting.

118
00:09:03.919 --> 00:09:06.919
And we're going to call this locomotion.

119
00:09:06.919 --> 00:09:17.769
So we know that this is going to give us our basic locomotion animations.

120
00:09:17.769 --> 00:09:25.389
Now, let's connect this up

121
00:09:25.389 --> 00:09:29.389
and double click on the state machine in order to open it up.

122
00:09:29.389 --> 00:09:37.519
Now again, this is going to give us a different type of graph.

123
00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:42.740
We will start off with the entry field on the left hand side here.

124
00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:48.029
And if you were to left click on the entry, drag off and drop down,

125
00:09:48.029 --> 00:09:53.029
you can see that you have three different options.

126
00:09:53.029 --> 00:09:57.090
Now we're going to work with states to start with.

127
00:09:57.090 --> 00:10:04.629
So if you select add state, it will add a state node.

128
00:10:04.629 --> 00:10:14.600
I'm going to call this idle to run.

129
00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:27.509
And the key to state machines is you can have multiple different states

130
00:10:27.509 --> 00:10:31.509
and define the methods of transitioning between them.

131
00:10:31.509 --> 00:10:38.509
So this will allow you to play different animations depending on what state you are in at any one time.

132
00:10:38.509 --> 00:10:43.240
To begin with, we're just going to work on the idle to run.

133
00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:48.690
So now that we've created this state, if you double click on it,

134
00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.690
you'll see that we're taken to another graph.

135
00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:56.690
And all that we have at this point is an output animation pose.

136
00:10:56.690 --> 00:11:03.559
What I'm going to search for in my asset browser is my walk cycle.

137
00:11:03.559 --> 00:11:06.559
So my BS.

138
00:11:06.559 --> 00:11:09.559
You can see that I can see it here in the browser.

139
00:11:09.559 --> 00:11:17.350
I will drag that in and connect that up.

140
00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:22.350
Now, once this has been dragged in, you can see that we have two input pins,

141
00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:26.350
speed of zero and none.

142
00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:28.350
Now we can ignore the none.

143
00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:33.350
However, we want the speed to be connected to the speed of the character.

144
00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:39.240
And we're going to do this by using the event graph.

145
00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:44.399
But to start with, we will need a variable to store this in.

146
00:11:44.399 --> 00:11:49.779
So if you come up to the green input pin for speed,

147
00:11:49.779 --> 00:11:57.000
right hand click, you can see at the top here, we have an option to promote to variable.

148
00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:05.769
If you left click that, you can now see that we have a green variable called speed.

149
00:12:05.769 --> 00:12:11.769
You will also see that we can see this in the variable section in the bottom left hand corner.

150
00:12:11.769 --> 00:12:22.419
Now we need to populate this variable and we can do that from the event graph.

151
00:12:22.419 --> 00:12:26.519
So I'm going to come up here and select the event graph.

152
00:12:26.519 --> 00:12:30.740
Now, when you first come into the event graph, we will have one event.

153
00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:37.740
And this event is the event blueprint update animation that we can see here slightly greyed out.

154
00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:43.769
Now, this is effectively tick for the animation.

155
00:12:43.769 --> 00:12:48.769
This is an event that will be fired by default on every frame of the game.

156
00:12:48.769 --> 00:12:55.769
And this allows us to do any of the logic which will be required in order to update the animation.

157
00:12:55.769 --> 00:13:00.029
I'm going to start off by using a different event.

158
00:13:00.029 --> 00:13:09.379
So I'm going to drag up. I'm going to hold down the right hand mouse button and search for initialise.

159
00:13:09.379 --> 00:13:15.379
And you can see at the top here we have event blueprint initialise animation.

160
00:13:15.379 --> 00:13:23.309
And this won't fire as often. It will only fire at the point that we are initialising starting up the blueprint.

161
00:13:23.309 --> 00:13:30.309
So this could be effectively very similar to the begin play node within blueprints.

162
00:13:30.309 --> 00:13:35.309
And the reason I'm using this is because a lot of the data that I want to get hold of,

163
00:13:35.309 --> 00:13:41.309
I'm going to need to get hold of from the movement component on our character.

164
00:13:41.309 --> 00:13:46.309
And I want to grab and save a reference to that movement component

165
00:13:46.309 --> 00:13:51.309
so that I'm not going to have to do a cast on every single frame of the game.

166
00:13:51.309 --> 00:14:04.690
So under this, I'm going to do a right hand click and search for get owning actor.

167
00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:12.690
And this is going to provide me with a reference to the actor which owns the skeletal mesh which this animation is playing.

168
00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:22.299
And I need a reference to the movement component.

169
00:14:22.299 --> 00:14:29.299
To do that, I'm going to have to cast this to the character class.

170
00:14:29.299 --> 00:14:39.990
So I'll type in cast to character here.

171
00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:44.639
And I'll plug that in.

172
00:14:44.639 --> 00:14:51.990
So a cast is going to say, is this actor of the class character?

173
00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:59.559
And if it is, do whatever is connected to this execution pin on the right here.

174
00:14:59.559 --> 00:15:06.460
And you can gain access to this class from the node here.

175
00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:10.460
And so what I'm going to do is I'm going to drag off of the as class.

176
00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:17.000
And I am going to search for character movement.

177
00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:22.159
And you will see the bottom here we have get character movement.

178
00:15:22.159 --> 00:15:28.320
And this character movement is going to provide me with some of the information I need to update the animation.

179
00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:35.960
So I will right hand click on the output pin and select promote to variable.

180
00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:48.470
And there you go. I now have a saved reference to the character movement.

181
00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:53.470
Now that's all I need to do on the Blueprint initialize animation at the moment.

182
00:15:53.470 --> 00:16:00.649
So let's jump down to the event blueprint update animation.

183
00:16:00.649 --> 00:16:05.649
And as I've mentioned before, this will run on tick.

184
00:16:05.649 --> 00:16:11.649
So this is going to run a lot, but it's great for getting information that needs to be used for that specific animation.

185
00:16:11.649 --> 00:16:20.080
So what I'm going to do is come down to the variable section and grab character movement.

186
00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:24.080
Drop it in. I'm going to select and get.

187
00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:31.639
So this will give me a node for character movement. And I want to calculate the speed.

188
00:16:31.639 --> 00:16:36.769
So the blend space knows what animation to output.

189
00:16:36.769 --> 00:16:42.769
In order to get the speed, I'm going to drag off and I'm going to get velocity.

190
00:16:42.769 --> 00:16:47.000
So if I type in get velocity.

191
00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:53.490
And you can see here right at the bottom, the yellow pin, we have get velocity.

192
00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:58.000
And because it's a yellow pin, that means it's providing us with a vector.

193
00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:01.000
So that's a struct of three floats.

194
00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:04.000
And a singular float is what we need with the blend space.

195
00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:15.119
So I'm going to drag off of this and I'm going to search for a node called length XY.

196
00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:21.650
And that's going to give me vector length XY. I'm going to select that.

197
00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:24.650
And you can see here this is returning a green pin.

198
00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:30.650
So this is a float, a singular number which allows decimal points.

199
00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:36.450
And so this is great. We can use this in our blend space.

200
00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:45.769
So I'm going to come down to the speed, drag that in and place it over the return value.

201
00:17:45.769 --> 00:17:51.769
And you'll be able to see we get a small little green tick to indicate that this would work.

202
00:17:51.769 --> 00:17:57.089
I'll drop it. And now you can see that we have the speed variable.

203
00:17:57.089 --> 00:18:02.240
I will plug that into the update animation.

204
00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:06.240
So what we're doing here is we are getting the movement component.

205
00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:09.240
We're finding out what its velocity is as a vector.

206
00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:14.240
We're then calculating the length of that vector only on X and Y.

207
00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:21.240
We're not interested in Z because that's up and down and we are saving that to the speed variable.

208
00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:27.720
So now if I was to push compile and save and jump back into the game and run,

209
00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:39.250
you would see that suddenly we have movement and the movement is dependent on the speed of our character at any one time.

210
00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:43.039
And that's looking really good.

211
00:18:43.039 --> 00:18:50.039
What you may have noticed is if you jump or fall, we have no animations that are connected to that at all.

212
00:18:50.039 --> 00:18:54.160
That's what we're going to have a look at in the next video.
